    <h2>Software Architect</h2>
    <article class="frontier-card frontier-card--gap">
      <header>
        <p>Architecture translation</p>
        <span>analogy · bounded</span>
      </header>
      <h2>Tables, views, transport, and the rendered interface</h2>
      <pre><code>{`derived relational store
    identities + typed relations + provenance

sector database view
    coefficient lens + admissible transformations

transport runtime
    gluing + specialization + continuation + coherence

UI view
    physical effects + support + normalization + observable record`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The Carrier may be compared to a typed identity–relation substrate. A sector constructs a
        governed view; transport keeps that view coherent across charts, boundaries, and parameter
        motion; the physical readout renders only distinctions that admitted effects can actually
        expose.
      </p>
      <p>
        The decisive warning is <strong>database decomposition is not physical
        distinguishability</strong>. A row, graph node, or algebraic summand is not an outcome
        merely because it can be stored separately. Shannon entropy appears only after the rendered
        physical view supplies operational alternatives and normalized weights.
      </p>
      <h3>Why Datomic is the closer analogy</h3>
      <p>
        Rich Hickey’s Datomic separates enduring entity identity from immutable database values
        and preserves provenance through transactions. Marici may similarly preserve labelled
        identity while sector and measurement contexts materialize coherent values. The analogy
        stops at a crucial boundary: Datomic values remain globally commutative and joinable,
        whereas two valid Marici views may be physically incompatible and lack one lawful global
        join.
      </p>
